{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786483918,"nonce":"n6pjZ+n3HTZdBoGcd3ab6VG1cTc=","title":"Title: `every authenticated route builds its own \u003cAuthenticated\u003e, so each navigation remounts AppShell and reopens the live stream`","message":"**What is wrong.** `web/src/App.tsx` gives each authenticated `\u003cRoute\u003e` its own inline element factory: `component={() =\u003e \u003cAuthenticated\u003e{\u003cAlertsRoute /\u003e}\u003c/Authenticated\u003e}` at `App.tsx:87`, and the same shape again at `:88-91`, `:92`, `:93-96` and `:97-100`. Five routes, five independent constructions of the same wrapper. `Authenticated` (`App.tsx:74-80`) is `RequireSession → LiveProvider → AppShell`, so navigating between any two of those five routes disposes that whole subtree and builds a fresh one. Solid has no reconciliation across sibling route components to save it; the shell is not shared, it is rebuilt.\n\nOnly the shell is rebuilt. `SessionProvider` sits in `Root` above the router (`App.tsx:63`) so the `/me` probe is not repeated, and `QueryClientProvider` is above the router too (`App.tsx:83`) so the query cache survives. What does not survive is component state inside the shell, and the SSE connection.\n\n**The demonstration.** The workaround for the first consequence is already in the tree, with a comment naming this defect. `SnoozeBanner`'s dismissal set had to be lifted out of the component into MODULE state — `const [dismissedSnoozes, setDismissedSnoozes] = createSignal(...)` at `web/src/components/ui/ShellBanner.tsx:208`, sitting at file scope. The comment above it, `ShellBanner.tsx:198-203`, reads: *\"⚠ It is module state because `AppShell` IS REMOUNTED ON EVERY ROUTE CHANGE — `App.tsx` gives each route its own `\u003cAuthenticated\u003e` wrapper instead of nesting the screens under one layout route, so component state here would reset on the first nav click and re-open the strip over the identical holds. Component state would pass its own regression test and still break for the operator.\"* The test file repeats it (`ShellBanner.test.tsx:111-113`) and ships `resetDismissedSnoozes` (`ShellBanner.tsx:215`) purely so one case's Dismiss click cannot silence the next case's strip. That export exists only because of this bug.\n\nThe second consequence is the stream. `LiveProvider` (`web/src/api/live.tsx:43`) constructs `new AlertStream(...)` at `:54` and, in `onMount`, subscribes and calls `stream.start()` (`live.tsx:92-98`), with `onCleanup` calling `stream.close()` (`live.tsx:100-104`). `close()` aborts the in-flight request, clears the stall interval and removes the `online`/`offline`/`visibilitychange` listeners (`web/src/api/stream.ts:248-261`); `start()` re-adds all of them and reconnects (`stream.ts:239-246`). Note this is not `EventSource` — the transport is `fetch` with a real `Last-Event-ID` header (`stream.ts:323`), which is what makes durable resume work at all. So every nav click closes one HTTP stream and opens another.\n\nWhat that reconnect costs, read off `stream.ts`: the new `AlertStream` restores the resume point from `sessionStorage` in its constructor (`stream.ts:200`), so `#connect` takes the `this.#lastSeq === null ? \"connecting\" : \"reconnecting\"` branch at `stream.ts:309` and reports **`reconnecting`** before the response headers arrive. `ConnectionBadge` renders that state as the word **Stale** (`web/src/components/AppShell.tsx:76-77`). The server then replays every `ui_events` row past the resume point, and each replayed frame runs through `invalidate` (`live.tsx:56-90`), so a navigation can also trigger cache invalidation for events already applied.\n\n**Why it matters.** The remount is cheap in wall-clock terms — the resume point is seconds old, so the replay set is normally tiny — and the honest defect is not performance. It is that the shell's own state is not durable across the navigation the shell exists to survive, and the repo has already paid for that once by moving a signal to module scope where it outlives every mount and needs a test-only reset hook.\n\nThe badge is the part worth stating carefully. `App.tsx:45-49` records an earlier fight with exactly this symptom: *\"the 'Stale, retry in 2s' badge the login gap used to leave on screen.\"* Every nav click now re-enters `reconnecting` and paints **Stale** in the header until the stream reattaches. It does *not* paint the countdown — the `· retry in` suffix needs `retryAt !== null` (`AppShell.tsx:101`), and only `#scheduleRetry` sets that (`stream.ts:397`). So this **plausibly** accounts for reports of a flickering Stale badge during navigation; it has not been proven to be the cause, and no measurement of the window is in hand.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/App.tsx:87-100` (the five per-route wrappers), `web/src/App.tsx:74-80` (`Authenticated`), `web/src/api/live.tsx:92-105` (mount/cleanup of the stream), `web/src/api/stream.ts:239-261`, `web/src/components/ui/ShellBanner.tsx:198-217` (the module-state workaround and its reset hook).\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** The five authenticated routes are children of one layout `\u003cRoute\u003e` whose component is `Authenticated`, so the shell is constructed once. A test navigates between two authenticated routes and asserts that `AppShell` is not remounted and that no second stream request is issued — the existing `stubFetch` harness can count requests to `/api/v1/stream`. `dismissedSnoozes` moves back inside `SnoozeBanner` and `resetDismissedSnoozes` is deleted, with `ShellBanner.test.tsx`'s dismissal cases still green.","files":null}]}